[h1]Better Commerce Screen UI — Changelog[/h1]

One bullet per change. Newest first.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.18[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Lighter on the game, above all during AI turns.[/b] This version is about performance: the mod asks the game fewer questions, repeats less work and lets go of what it no longer needs. What it decides and shows is meant to stay exactly the same.
[*][b]Automatic assignment and Hands off ignore other players.[/b] Another empire's new settlement, resource slots or notifications no longer wake them up while the AI takes its turn.
[*][b]A full empire costs nothing to wait on.[/b] With automatic assignment on and every settlement full, a resource waiting for a slot no longer recalculates your whole empire every 15 seconds.
[*][b]Assigning resources is cheaper.[/b] Assign All, Reassign All and automatic assignment make fewer calls into the game per resource, and the Resource Allocation button on the HUD is re-checked once the run is over instead of after every resource placed.
[*][b]Treasure Convoys sailing home cost less at the start of each turn.[/b]
[*][b]The Trade Routes tab opens, redraws and closes with less work.[/b] Its setup and cleanup run once instead of once per route card, sorting and filtering score each route once, and every redraw asks the game less.
[*][b]The Commerce screen opens lighter with many settlements.[/b] A settlement's priority menu is built the first time you open it, and the Empire and Factory tabs prepare a resource's tooltip the first time you hover it.
[*][b]No more tooltips left behind in memory[/b] after collapsing and reopening a section, while the GDP figure updates during Assign All, or after closing the screen.
[*][b]Holding Shift to preview resources does less work per frame,[/b] and padlocks switched off in the options cost nothing while the Resources tab is open.
[*][b]Queued trade actions buy their Merchant as soon as you can afford one,[/b] even if you never open the Commerce screen in between. They used to go by the prices from the last time the tab was open.
[*][b]Fixed: the "one trade slot away" group lost its header[/b] after you sent or queued a Merchant, or when you came back to the tab.
[*][b]Fixed: a queued request on a card held back by the trade limit could leave that card with no buttons at all.[/b]
[*][b]Fixed: the buy buttons could mix up two foreign settlements that share an internal number,[/b] naming the wrong one to buy from.
[*][b]Fixed: the GDP breakdown counted resources from a city you had since captured as imports.[/b]
[*][b]Fixed: trade route names were not shortened before the leader portrait[/b] while the "one trade slot away" group was collapsed or filtered out.
[*][b]Fixed: opening the Commerce screen or the Trade Routes tab crashed the game[/b] after Civilization VII 1.5.0.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.17[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Fixed: no hourglass on a card whose leader you are hostile with.[/b] The card proposed "Improve Trade Relations" happily while you could afford it, then refused to let you PLAN the same thing once the price rose past your Influence - so the button went dark instead of offering to do it on a later turn. Hostility does not shut the treaty: the game still allows the proposal and asks only for the Influence. The hourglass now follows the game's own refusal, and appears wherever a turn is all that is missing.
[*][b]The resources on a trade route card stop repeating where they come from.[/b] The import banner tinted with the other empire's colours, and the "Origin:" line in the resource's tooltip, both answered a question the card answers across its own title - every resource on such a card is imported, and from that one settlement. The resource, its kind and what it pays are untouched.
[*][b]No dark buttons on the trade cards any more.[/b] Where a card can neither start the route now nor offer to plan it, it draws no button at all instead of a dimmed attention mark - planning with the hourglass has taken that button's place.
[*][b]The relationship icon is gone from the refusal about Influence.[/b] "You need 150 Influence but only have 44" is not about relations, and the mark on the end of it named the wrong obstacle.
[*][b]Fixed: a Merchant you could not afford sometimes offered no hourglass either.[/b] The game does not always flag a purchase as unaffordable when it refuses it, so the price is now compared against your Gold as well.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.16[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Fixed: the trade route cards lost every button.[/b] A mistake in 1.15 left the Trade Routes tab with no gold button, no plus, no pin and no cancel mark, and the "Free Merchants" line blank. Sorry.
[*][b]Plan any route you cannot start yet, not just the ones held back by the trade limit.[/b] The hourglass now appears wherever waiting would help: no spare Merchant, not enough Gold to buy one, or a limit that needs raising first. Press it and the mod buys or sends when it can, then raises the limit once the Influence is there.
[*][b]One button per card instead of two.[/b] The price button grows its own hourglass rather than standing beside a second control repeating the same cost - and it stays fully lit, because it still answers a click.
[*][b]A spare Merchant now leaves the moment you plan the route,[/b] instead of waiting for the turn to turn. Six routes planned while four Merchants stood about was four wasted turns of travel. Buying still waits for the turn, so Gold never leaves your treasury on a click.
[*][b]A planned route no longer proposes a treaty it does not need.[/b] If a trade slot is already free by the time the Merchant is on its way, the Influence is left alone.
[*][b]"Unavailable trade routes" now means what it says.[/b] Routes held back by nothing but the trade limit sit under the available ones instead - they are a decision you can buy your way out of, not a dead end. What stays unavailable is out of range, or a leader too hostile to raise the limit with at all.
[*][b]No planning offered where waiting cannot help.[/b] Hostile relations shut the treaty, and no number of turns opens it - so those cards say so instead of promising something that will never come. Whether a war stops a route is left to the game, which knows the civilizations that trade through one.
[*][b]The cards stop listing what is already fine.[/b] "In range ✔" and "At peace ✔" said nothing you were asking about; only the line in red remains.
[*][b]Fixed: filtering trade routes by yield was wrong in both directions.[/b] Ivory pays Production and the Production filter hid it; Iron, Niter and Horses pay Combat Strength and it showed them. The filter now reads what each resource actually does - the same source as the figures on the Empire tab - so it agrees with the tooltips on the cards. Influence works as a filter too.
[*][b]Fixed: opening the tab could claim you could not afford a Merchant when you could.[/b] The game answers that question wrongly for a moment as the screen opens; the prices are now asked again once it has settled.
[*][b]The refusal about relationships carries the relationship's own icon.[/b]
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.15[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]New: plan a Merchant errand for when the turn turns.[/b] Where "Improve Trade Relations" cannot be proposed right now - you have already proposed it this turn, or you are short of Influence - the card offers an hourglass. Press it and the mod does the whole thing for you: a spare Merchant sets off for that settlement at once, and the treaty is proposed as soon as you can afford it. The Merchant spends the wait covering ground it would have had to cover anyway. Press the X beside the hourglass to call the whole thing off.
[*][b]New: two notifications for it.[/b] One when the Merchant sets off, one when the limit actually rises - separate messages, because they can be many turns apart.
[*][b]New: a Trade Routes button on the dock,[/b] right of Resource Allocation, opening the Commerce screen straight onto the Trade Routes tab.
[*][b]New: a "Trade routes being established" section,[/b] between the routes you are running and the ones you could start. Every route with a Merchant already coming, or a plan queued against it, lives there - and drops back where it came from the moment you cancel.
[*][b]New: "Free Merchants" under the trade route total,[/b] so you can see at a glance whether there is anyone to send.
[*][b]New: three figures at the foot of a leader's relationship tooltip[/b] - your trade route limit with them, the routes running, and your Merchants on their way. The tooltip is narrower too, and no longer runs across the cards it opens from.
[*][b]Fixed: a Merchant would not open its route after arriving.[/b] One arriving on its last movement point cannot sign the route that turn, and the mod read that as "the player called it back" and threw the errand away - leaving the Merchant standing at the gate for the rest of the game.
[*][b]Fixed: one Merchant could be given two errands.[/b] A Merchant told to go somewhere but not yet moving still counted as free, so the next thing that wanted one took it and quietly overwrote its orders. The route it had been promised to was never opened.
[*][b]Fixed: sending a spare Merchant left the card looking untouched[/b] - no pin, no cancel mark, and the plus still offering the Merchant that had just been sent.
[*][b]Fixed: a card and its own section could disagree about the same Merchant.[/b] One that had arrived and was waiting for a trade slot lost its pin and its cancel mark, and the card offered to buy a second one.
[*][b]Fixed: a card offered to buy a Merchant for a slot already spoken for.[/b] The trade limit is counted per leader, so with two Merchants already sent to one empire there is nothing left for a third - the price and the plus now know that.
[*][b]Fixed: opening the screen straight onto Trade Routes drew the tab strip as words[/b] before swapping in the icons, and left the underline under the wrong tab.
[*][b]Fixed: the queue could announce a Merchant it had not sent.[/b]
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.14[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Fixed: the GDP figure counted points you had not unlocked yet.[/b] Every economic scoring source has to be switched on by a technology or a civic first - the Wheel for City and Bonus Resources, Currency for Gold buildings, Skilled Trades for imported Resources, Mass Production for Factory Resources - and the mod paid them out regardless. A source you have not unlocked now counts as zero, and its line in the tooltip says in red what it is waiting for.
[*][b]New: "Do not touch my resources".[/b] A new choice on the automatic-assignment setting. The game slots a newly improved Resource into the settlement that improved it as soon as that settlement has a free slot, without asking - this sends it straight back to the unassigned pool so you place every Resource yourself. Resources that come with a captured settlement are left alone.
[*][b]New: the "≈" figure on the Factory tab shows its working.[/b] Hover it for the base the percentage is taken from, the Resource's percentage, and what the two come to.
[*][b]Fixed: the Factory tab's "≈" estimates were too high.[/b] They took the percentage of the figure in the top panel, but the game adds its percentage bonuses together and takes them all from the same base - so five Tea at +15% Science is 15% of your settlements' output, not 15% of a total that already carries a +25% from somewhere else. The estimates now read from the settlements. Reported by a player on Steam, with the numbers to prove it - thank you.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.13[/h2]

[list]
[*][b]Fixed: this mod could wipe other mods' settings.[/b] Its per-game data (settlement priorities and merchant orders) sat in its own entries in browser storage instead of the shared "modSettings" record every other mod uses, and several popular mods erase the whole store the moment they see anything else in there - taking everyone's settings with it, including their own. Everything now lives inside the shared record under this mod's own name, and the old entries are cleaned up on first load. Your own priorities and orders carry across.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.12[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Fixed: "Reassign All" could leave Resources in the pool with empty slots all over your empire.[/b] Three causes, all in the same path. Emptying the empire came back before the game had finished doing it, so the layout that followed ran against a board that was still changing and stopped early. A Resource the game turned down in ONE settlement was set aside for the whole pass instead of just for that settlement, so it was never offered anywhere else. And a single operation the game would not accept ended the run outright, abandoning everything after it.
[*][b]A Treasure Convoy that sails home by itself now says so.[/b] A short line appears at the top of the screen when one unloads, naming the GDP and Gold it just paid out. Automatic return was silent before this — the only sign of it was the convoy no longer being on the map.
[*][b]Several convoys arriving together are announced once, with the totals[/b], rather than as a stack of lines replacing each other faster than any of them could be read.
[*]The figures are the same ones the Treasure Convoys tab already prints on the card, so the two cannot disagree. Nothing about the return itself changed, and the line only appears when [b]Send convoys home[/b] is on.
[*][b]Fixed: the mod reported itself as version 1.10.[/b]
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.11[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing on screen changes and no setting moves.[/b] The third sweep over what the mod costs, aimed at the part that was always going to be the largest: laying out a whole empire.
[*][b]Laying out an empire no longer re-scores the whole empire for every resource it places.[/b] Each choice is still made against the board the last one left behind — but the answers for every OTHER settlement were being thrown away each time, and now only the settlement a resource landed in is recomputed.
[*][b]The board is carried instead of rebuilt for every resource.[/b] Reading every settlement out of the game before every single placement, to reflect a change in one of them, was the other half of the cost. The whole board is still re-read once a second, so nothing that moves your empire from outside goes unnoticed.
[*][b]Dropping one settlement's answers no longer walks every answer the planner is holding[/b] — it was a comparison against the size of the pool times the size of your empire, once per resource placed.
[*][b]Waiting for the game to accept a resource backs off[/b] instead of asking every four milliseconds until it does.
[*][b]The mod indexes the game's modifier tables for resources only.[/b] Around 12 000 modifiers ship in the game's data; a few hundred belong to resources, and the mod was building and keeping indexes over all of it.
[*][b]Fixed: moving to the next age left the previous age's answers in memory.[/b] The game's resource, unit and modifier tables are the age's own — whether Gold is an Empire resource or a Treasure one changes with it — and every cache the mod builds from them now empties when the age ends.
[*][b]A Treasure Convoy pass no longer asks the game about units it has already ruled out[/b], once per tile of every unit you move.
[*][b]A trade route card is matched to its route once per pass instead of twice.[/b]
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.10[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Nothing on screen changes and no setting moves.[/b] Like 1.9, this is about what the mod costs the game — the second sweep over the same ground, because 1.9 fixed the subscriptions and left the work behind them alone.
[*][b]Fixed: a Treasure Convoy asked the pathfinder about every plot you own.[/b] The same mistake 1.9 fixed for Merchants, in the module nobody looked at afterwards — four to eight hundred full pathfinder queries per convoy, three times a turn, at the moment the turn begins. Most of them were land plots, which a naval unit can never enter, and a search that fails is the expensive one. Automatic return is on by default, so this was running for everybody.
[*][b]Settlement priorities are no longer read from disk tens of thousands of times per layout.[/b] A settlement you never set a priority on was the one answer the mod refused to remember, and the scoring asks once per resource-and-settlement pair for every single resource it places.
[*][b]Three more answers were being worked out again for every pair[/b] — whether a conditional bonus applies here, which settlement a requirement is about, and what a settlement's yields stand at.
[*][b]Emptying settlements no longer waits half a second for messages that are not coming[/b], once per resource and once per settlement.
[*][b]Fixed: a wait for your own resource could be ended by another empire's[/b], letting the next step read a board that had not finished changing.
[*][b]Fixed: a padlock on an imported Resource was never dropped when the Resource left.[/b] The mod was filing its own events as somebody else's, because it read the owner of the plot rather than the settlement the event names.
[*][b]Fixed: the Commerce screen could end up disagreeing with the game after a bulk assignment.[/b] Its own model applies one resource per event and keeps only the latest when several arrive together, so tiles went missing from settlement cards. The mod used to give the display a frame after every single placement to try to keep up — a hundred redraws over a full empire, and it did not fix it. The run now goes at full speed and the whole board is put right once, at the end, cards included.
[*][b]The Commerce screen stopped re-asking the game for answers that never change.[/b] Icons were fetched every time one was drawn; the padlock on every assigned Resource composed its own tooltip every frame; each settlement card rebuilt the eight priority names and their explanations from scratch; the GDP figure re-counted every building in the empire several times a second; and Shift was read from the engine on every mouse movement. All of it is remembered now.
[*][b]The Trade Routes tab stops listening when you leave it[/b], instead of keeping five engine subscriptions for the rest of the session.
[*][b]A trade route card asked the same three questions of your whole army[/b] — a tab of forty cards walked it over a hundred times per redraw. It is one walk now.
[*][b]The Empire and Factory tabs no longer rebuild a static index on every visit[/b], and the layout watcher stops watching the whole HUD once it has what it was waiting for.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.9[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]One new option; otherwise nothing on screen changes.[/b] This release is about what the mod costs the game — every other feature behaves exactly as it did in 1.8.
[*][b]New: "Don't show tooltips on the Commerce screen"[/b] (Options → Mods, off by default). Hides every tooltip this mod draws, and the round "?" marks with them. The game's own tooltips are untouched.
[*][b]Fixed: a Merchant on a standing order could add a minute to the turn.[/b] It asked the pathfinder about every plot its target owns — thirty to fifty — three times a turn, and a target it could not reach was the most expensive question of all. It now probes the ten nearest.
[*][b]The mod no longer listens to other empires' turns.[/b] Unit movement, resource assignment and building events are raised by the engine for every player in the game, not for you — thousands of them per AI turn in a late game — and this mod woke up on all of them. Every subscription is now filtered by whose event it is before any work starts.
[*][b]Automatic assignment installs nothing while it is switched off[/b], and off is the default. It used to subscribe to a dozen engine events and sweep every fifteen seconds whatever the setting said.
[*][b]Merchant orders and Treasure Convoys stop scanning your whole army.[/b] A pass now runs only when something is actually under an order, and convoy events check the one unit they name.
[*][b]Four observers watching the entire HUD became one watching the Commerce screen[/b], running once per frame instead of once per DOM change anywhere in the game.
[*][b]The HUD's Resource Allocation button no longer recomputes on other empires' assignments[/b] — that check is the most expensive thing this mod does.
[*][b]Fixed: leaks that grew for as long as the session did.[/b] The GDP readout, the settlement cards and the tab strip each left listeners or observers behind on every visit to the Commerce screen.
[*][b]Fixed: automatic assignment could not resume after being blocked.[/b] A trigger arriving while the Commerce screen was open was meant to be held and retried; it was being lost instead.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.8[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Send a spare Merchant[/b] — a plus beside the price on a route card, shown only while you actually have a Merchant with nothing to do (one left over from an earlier Age, say). Sends that one instead of buying another; the plus disappears everywhere the moment it is spoken for.
[*][b]A Merchant bought for a route it could already open no longer walks off.[/b] From the Modern Age it can open a route from where it stands — it only has to wait for next turn, because a unit bought this turn has no movement. It now waits instead of setting off across the map.
[*][b]The settlement card's return button reads "Unassign all in Berlin"[/b], matching the buttons at the top of the screen, instead of the game's oddly-phrased "Return all assignments from the city of Berlin".
[*][b]Every tooltip this mod adds is now the game's framed one[/b] — settlement controls, the factory button and the tab icons included. A tab icon's tooltip says what is on that screen rather than repeating the tab's name twice.
[*][b]A card with a Merchant already on the way drops the price[/b] and the locate button shows how many turns until it arrives.
[*][b]An X beside the locate pin calls a Merchant off[/b] — stops the journey and cancels the errand, without leaving the Commerce screen. It keeps its remaining movement and is free to be sent elsewhere.
[*][b]Merchants in Antiquity and Exploration now actually set off.[/b] The turn-begin event fires before units get their movement back, so the one pass allowed to start a journey always saw a Merchant with nothing to travel on. The mod now also acts when movement is restored.
[*][b]The age rule is explicit:[/b] Antiquity and Exploration, the Merchant must reach the settlement; Modern, it opens the route from anywhere once it has movement — and never travels needlessly.
[*][b]Cancelling a Merchant's journey now clears its order in the Modern age too[/b], so the plus button offers it straight away.
[*][b]A Merchant bought this turn can be given a job the same turn[/b] — the plus buttons no longer treat "no movement left" as busy. The route opens when the turn begins.
[*][b]The "raise the limit and send" button no longer walks the Merchant across the map[/b] — the treaty it proposes takes a moment to take effect, and the Merchant used to set off before it did.
[*][b]A Merchant is judged by what it is doing[/b], not by what the mod wrote down — so the plus appears after reloading a save, and a Merchant you call back stops being reported as "already on its way", and drops its standing order instead of being re-sent next turn.
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] that button ignored resource locks — a padlocked Resource was swept away by it even though "Unassign all" spared it.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.7[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Proposing "Improve Trade Relations" redraws the whole tab[/b], not just the button clicked. A trade limit is per leader, so every card of that leader, the group headers and the total above the tabs all went stale.
[*][b]The propose button goes dark the moment it is pressed.[/b] It can only be used once per turn per leader, and staying bright and priced made the feature look broken.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.6[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Per-resource locks.[/b] A padlock on any assigned Resource keeps it in place through [b]Unassign all[/b] and [b]Reassign all[/b]. Locks survive a reload, and can be switched off entirely in Options → Mods.
[*][b]Treasure Convoys can send themselves home[/b] — on by default, with a checkbox on the Treasure tab. A loaded convoy sails to your nearest Homeland Settlement and unloads at the first tile of your own territory it reaches. Steer it yourself and it stays where you put it.
[*][b]Shift moves a whole kind of Resource in every direction[/b], not only out of the pool — settlement to settlement and settlement to pool now work too.
[*][b]The HUD's Resource Allocation button says when it is worth opening[/b] — full colour once assignment is unlocked, and a gentle pulse while something in the pool would actually be accepted somewhere. Built to sit alongside [b]Ready or Not[/b].
[*][b]Trade route cards laid out in three plain pieces[/b]: name and prices on one row, resources below, portrait in its own corner.
[*][b]"Return the factory resources" is one button[/b] instead of three things wide.
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] Shift-clicking stopped doing anything at all; clicks beside a Resource behaved as clicks on it.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.5[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Blocked only by the trade limit?[/b] One button proposes "Improve Trade Relations" with that leader [i]and[/i] buys and sends a Merchant regardless of the answer — priced in both Influence and Gold up front.
[*][b]The capacity warning can now fix what it warns about[/b], proposing the same treaty straight from the card.
[*][b]A blocked route's title tooltip says why[/b] — out of range, at war, or capacity full, in the game's own wording.
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] "Factories first" ignored how many copies of each Factory Resource the pool held; buying a Merchant killed the tooltips on other cards; the Trade Routes tab could be dragged sideways.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.4[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Buy and send a Merchant straight from a trade route card.[/b] One click buys it in the right settlement, walks it there, and opens the route itself.
[*][b]Filter tabs above each trade route section[/b] — show only routes carrying a given yield, richest first.
[*][b]The "unavailable trade routes" section opens by default[/b], split into "one trade slot away" and "out of range".
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] Hardwood's card showed raw markup; the "no Trade Route slot" tooltip named the wrong fix.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.3[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]"Imports first"[/b] and [b]"Prioritise Happiness"[/b] switches, a [b]running GDP total[/b] toward the Economic Victory, and an option to build a Culture Settlement automatically.
[*][b]Every tooltip this mod adds is drawn in the game's framed style[/b], including the Empire and Factory tabs.
[*][b]"Balanced" now means Production in a City and Food in a Town[/b], and a Resource is no longer called "specially suited" to a settlement that only gets its weaker half.
[*][b]Automatic assignment runs the same code the buttons run[/b], and no longer loses a trigger that arrives at a bad moment.
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] several cases where the end-turn prompt nagged about Resources that had nowhere to go.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.2[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]The "Resource Assignments Available" prompt no longer takes over the turn button[/b] when every settlement is full and nothing new can go anywhere. It returns as soon as something can actually be placed.
[*][b]Empire resources:[/b] the combat column sizes itself to its widest card instead of always taking a third of the screen, cards are centred, and the figures line up in columns.
[*][b]Factory resources:[/b] cards centred to match, four to a row instead of three, and the origin tooltip totals each leader's copies beside their name.
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] automatic assignment could miss a resource and wait until the next turn — the event announcing a finished improvement fires before the resource is in your hands, so a single look found nothing. It now looks again over the next few seconds, and a pass that placed nothing no longer counts the arrival as handled.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]1.1[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]The remaining languages[/b] (machine-translated).
[*][b]Fixed:[/b] minor bugs.
[/list]
